  "released": "2026-08-14",
  "changelog": "v167: focus autopilot \u2014 the Convergence Principle's floor applied to focus creation (ADR 0011, implementing ADR 0006). Until now a passive user's system NEVER grew a new Focus on its own \u2014 approval was the only path, a gap ADR 0006 named outright. The weekly run now keeps AUTOPILOT_TARGET_DEVELOPING (3) focuses in active, non-primary, unsaturated development: when that \"developing\" set thins below target and a pending Focus idea scores at/above FOCUS_READY_SCORE_FLOOR (8.0), the system approves it itself through the exact same approve_recommendation() path a manual approval takes \u2014 category scaffolding, roadmap registration, and starter-question seeding (including the existing keyless emit-task fallback) ride along for free, never a parallel scaffold path. Gentle by default: AUTOPILOT_MAX_PER_RUN (1) auto-approval per weekly run, so the owner's \"keep 3 in development\" end-state is reached over successive weeks; a new `--catch-up` CLI flag fills to target in one run for the everything-answered case. Approvals are now provenance-stamped `approved_by`: \"owner\" for every manual approval going forward, \"auto\" for autopilot, absent on records approved before this PR (legacy). New `focus-autopilot [--dry-run] [--target N] [--catch-up]` CLI command and `weekly_maintenance.sh` learning step (after candidate auto-promotion and queue planning \u2014 a newly-approved Focus's seeded questions enter NEXT week's planning, a documented one-run lag mirroring ADR 0009's). The Review lane's focus-ideas policy line drops \"never created without you\" for the honest new posture, numbers read live from the module constants. See ADR 0011.",
